There are around 450 malls listed there, submitted as sort of oral histories. In particular, what's interesting, I think, about deadmalls.com is how nostalgic a lot of this is, and it does make sense. I mean, in so many suburban communities, the mall became the de facto town center. It was really the center of social life other than the school.
那里列出了大约450个商场，作为口述历史提交。我认为，关于deadmalls.com特别有趣的是，其中有多少怀旧情绪，这确实有道理。我的意思是，在这么多郊区社区，商场成了事实上的城镇中心。除了学校之外，它确实是社交生活的中心。

28
00:13:51.000 --> 00:14:35.000
I would be very sad if all of Victor Groen's malls were demolished. We should certainly work to preserve at least one. The most famous mall in Minnesota may be the mall of America. With its roller coaster, it's zipline, it's aquarium and water park. But the most architecturally significant mall, its grandfather, is the one that's just a 12-minute drive away in a diner. Jeff Hardwick's book is called Mallmaker, Victor Groen architect of an American Dream. An Ellen Dunham Jones book is called Retro Fitting Suburban, special thanks to Claire Dordy for research help.
如果维克多·格鲁恩所有的商场都被拆除，我会非常难过。我们当然应该努力至少保留一个。明尼苏达州最著名的商场可能是美国商场。有它的过山车、滑索、水族馆和水上乐园。但建筑上最重要的商场，它的祖父，是那个在伊迪纳开车只需12分钟的地方。杰夫·哈德威克的书叫《商场制造者：维克多·格鲁恩，美国梦的建筑师》。艾伦·邓纳姆·琼斯的书叫《郊区改造》，特别感谢克莱尔·多迪提供的研究帮助。

